WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.630
- [Evan] What's up,
Believe Nation? It's Evan.

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:03.833
My one word is believe and I believe

00:00:03.833 --> 00:00:06.328
in people more than they
believe in themselves.

00:00:06.328 --> 00:00:07.963
And my sincere hope is that if you

00:00:07.963 --> 00:00:11.351
see in yourself what I see in you,

00:00:11.351 --> 00:00:13.617
you'll be able to change the planet.

00:00:13.617 --> 00:00:14.715
And so to help you on your journey

00:00:14.715 --> 00:00:16.291
today I'm continuing my series

00:00:16.291 --> 00:00:18.465
on "Success: Advice from the Top,

00:00:18.465 --> 00:00:22.312
How Billionaires Think" volume number two.

00:00:22.312 --> 00:00:23.589
And as always as you're
watching if you hear

00:00:23.589 --> 00:00:25.110
something that really resonates with you,

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.732
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:26.732 --> 00:00:27.989
and put quotes around it so other

00:00:27.989 --> 00:00:29.084
people can enjoy and

00:00:29.084 --> 00:00:30.439
when you write it down, it's more likely

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:32.308
to stick with yourself as well.

00:00:32.308 --> 00:00:33.849
Enjoy.

00:00:33.849 --> 00:00:36.682
(Whooshing noise)

00:00:37.982 --> 00:00:41.315
(light piano interlude)

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:45.380
- I say to my girls all of the time,

00:00:46.407 --> 00:00:47.990
that your real work

00:00:49.063 --> 00:00:50.396
is to figure out

00:00:51.608 --> 00:00:53.691
where your power base is.

00:00:55.324 --> 00:00:58.310
And to work on the alignment

00:00:58.310 --> 00:00:59.977
of your personality,

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.325
your gifts that you have to give,

00:01:03.325 --> 00:01:05.636
with the real reason why you're here.

00:01:05.636 --> 00:01:08.757
That's the number one
thing you have to do,

00:01:08.757 --> 00:01:12.943
is to work on yourself
and to fill yourself

00:01:12.943 --> 00:01:15.110
up and keep your cup full.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.221
Keep yourself full. Now I
used to be afraid of that.

00:01:20.221 --> 00:01:21.548
I used to afraid.

00:01:21.548 --> 00:01:22.687
Particularly from people who'd say,

00:01:22.687 --> 00:01:24.254
"Oh, she's so full of herself,

00:01:24.254 --> 00:01:25.631
mmm, she's so full of herself,"

00:01:25.631 --> 00:01:27.152
(laughter)

00:01:27.152 --> 00:01:28.902
and now I embrace it.

00:01:30.032 --> 00:01:34.199
I consider it a compliment
that I am full of myself.

00:01:35.102 --> 00:01:37.687
Because only when you're full,

00:01:37.687 --> 00:01:40.954
I'm full, I'm overflowing,
my cup runneth over.

00:01:40.954 --> 00:01:44.081
I have so much to offer
and so much to give,

00:01:44.081 --> 00:01:47.331
and I am not afraid of honoring myself.

00:01:49.046 --> 00:01:53.685
You know? It's miraculous
when you think about it.

00:01:53.685 --> 00:01:57.018
(light piano interlude)

00:01:58.396 --> 00:02:00.223
- Sometimes being young and trying things,

00:02:00.223 --> 00:02:02.003
you're so naive and you
don't know any better,

00:02:02.003 --> 00:02:03.354
all you do is learn.

00:02:03.354 --> 00:02:05.264
And if you fail, it doesn't matter.

00:02:05.264 --> 00:02:09.137
And so whether I was nine, ten,
twelve, sixteen, twenty one,

00:02:09.137 --> 00:02:11.354
the failures were irrelevant.

00:02:11.354 --> 00:02:13.404
And you know, whether you're
nine, twelve, sixteen,

00:02:13.404 --> 00:02:15.408
twenty one, twenty two, twenty four,

00:02:15.408 --> 00:02:17.961
you know, I'm sleeping
on the couch, you know?

00:02:17.961 --> 00:02:20.133
I have a car with a
hole in the floorboard.

00:02:20.133 --> 00:02:23.151
You know, I'm living like
a bum and like a student.

00:02:23.151 --> 00:02:25.038
And so what did I have to lose?

00:02:25.038 --> 00:02:28.262
And so I think that influenced
me as much as anything.

00:02:28.262 --> 00:02:31.509
You know, my dad was like,
"go for it." You know?

00:02:31.509 --> 00:02:33.837
Like, why not? What have you got to lose?

00:02:33.837 --> 00:02:37.059
You'll have to sleep on a different couch?

00:02:37.059 --> 00:02:39.725
So I think that that was motivation.

00:02:39.725 --> 00:02:43.058
(light piano interlude)

00:02:44.387 --> 00:02:48.502
- I have an old-fashioned
belief that I can

00:02:48.502 --> 00:02:50.484
only should expect to make money

00:02:50.484 --> 00:02:51.738
in things that I understand.

00:02:51.738 --> 00:02:55.346
And when I say understand,
I don't mean understand

00:02:55.346 --> 00:02:58.141
you know, what a product
does or anything like that.

00:02:58.141 --> 00:03:00.779
I mean understand what the
economics of the business

00:03:00.779 --> 00:03:03.356
are likely to look at,
look like, ten years

00:03:03.356 --> 00:03:05.387
from now or twenty years from now.

00:03:05.387 --> 00:03:08.460
I know in general, what the economics

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.258
of say, Wrigley chewing gum will look like

00:03:10.258 --> 00:03:11.776
ten years from now.

00:03:11.776 --> 00:03:15.402
The internet isn't going to
change the way people chew gum.

00:03:15.402 --> 00:03:17.769
It isn't going to change
which gum they chew.

00:03:17.769 --> 00:03:22.272
If you own the chewing
gum market, in a big way,

00:03:22.272 --> 00:03:25.789
and you've got DoubleMint and
SpearMint and Juicy Fruit,

00:03:25.789 --> 00:03:28.318
those brands will be
there ten years from now.

00:03:28.318 --> 00:03:31.287
I can't pinpoint exactly what the numbers

00:03:31.287 --> 00:03:32.511
are going to look like on Wrigley,

00:03:32.511 --> 00:03:34.760
but I'm not going to be way
off if I try to look forward

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.151
on something like that.

00:03:36.151 --> 00:03:39.038
Evaluating that company
is within what I call my

00:03:39.038 --> 00:03:40.328
"circle of competence".

00:03:40.328 --> 00:03:43.810
I understand what they do, I
understand the economics of it,

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.917
I understand the competitive
aspects of the business.

00:03:46.917 --> 00:03:50.750
So figuring out the
economic consequences, tv,

00:03:52.304 --> 00:03:54.792
I think I know there's 20,
25 million sets a year sold

00:03:54.792 --> 00:03:56.362
in the United States.

00:03:56.362 --> 00:03:57.689
I don't think there's one of them made

00:03:57.689 --> 00:03:59.148
in the United States anymore.

00:03:59.148 --> 00:04:02.990
You'd say, tv set manufacturer,
what a wonderful business,

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:05.292
nobody had a tv in 1950.

00:04:05.292 --> 00:04:08.055
Thereabouts forty five, to fifty.

00:04:08.055 --> 00:04:10.177
Everybody has multiple sets now,

00:04:10.177 --> 00:04:13.551
nobody in the United States
has made any real money,

00:04:13.551 --> 00:04:15.252
making the sense that
they're all out of business.

00:04:15.252 --> 00:04:18.492
You know, the Magnavoxes, the
RCAs, all of those companies.

00:04:18.492 --> 00:04:20.380
Radio was the equivalent of the twenty.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:23.283
Over 500 companies making
radios in the 1920s.

00:04:23.283 --> 00:04:25.877
And again I don't think
there's a US radio manufacturer

00:04:25.877 --> 00:04:27.623
at the present time.

00:04:27.623 --> 00:04:30.160
But Coca-Cola, you know, what was it,

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.489
1884 at Jacob's Pharmacy or whatever,

00:04:32.489 --> 00:04:34.349
the fellow comes up with something,

00:04:34.349 --> 00:04:36.305
a lot of copiers over the years,

00:04:36.305 --> 00:04:40.228
but now you've got a
company that is selling

00:04:40.228 --> 00:04:44.663
roughly 1.1 billion eight
ounce servings of its product,

00:04:44.663 --> 00:04:47.843
Not all Coke, Sprite
and some others, daily

00:04:47.843 --> 00:04:50.845
throughout the world, 117 years later.

00:04:50.845 --> 00:04:55.463
So understanding the economic
characteristics of a business

00:04:55.463 --> 00:04:57.976
is different than predicting
the fact that an industry

00:04:57.976 --> 00:04:59.668
is going to do wonderfully.

00:04:59.668 --> 00:05:02.918
And so I look at the internet business,

00:05:04.067 --> 00:05:05.471
or I look at tech messes,

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:07.024
I say this is a marvelous thing,

00:05:07.024 --> 00:05:08.941
and I love to play around on the computer,

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:11.794
I order my books from Amazon
and all kinds of things.

00:05:11.794 --> 00:05:13.612
But I don't know who's going to win.

00:05:13.612 --> 00:05:15.368
Unless I know who's going to win,

00:05:15.368 --> 00:05:16.702
I'm not interested in investing.

00:05:16.702 --> 00:05:18.037
I'll just play around on the computer.

00:05:18.037 --> 00:05:20.548
(laughter)

00:05:20.548 --> 00:05:22.715
Defining your circle of competence

00:05:22.715 --> 00:05:25.723
is the most important aspect of investing.

00:05:25.723 --> 00:05:28.556
It's not how large your circle is,

00:05:29.681 --> 00:05:32.026
you don't have to be an
expert on everything.

00:05:32.026 --> 00:05:34.159
But knowing where the
perimeter of that circle

00:05:34.159 --> 00:05:36.440
of what you know and
what you don't know is,

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:40.120
and staying inside of
it, is all important.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:44.025
Tom Watson Sr., who started
IBM, said in his book,

00:05:44.025 --> 00:05:48.398
he said, "I'm no genius.
But I'm smart in spots,

00:05:48.398 --> 00:05:49.711
and I stay around those spots."

00:05:49.711 --> 00:05:50.640
(laughter)

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.060
and you know that is the key.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:54.688
So if I understand a few things,

00:05:54.688 --> 00:05:57.909
and I stick in that arena, I'll do okay.

00:05:57.909 --> 00:05:59.554
And if I don't understand something

00:05:59.554 --> 00:06:00.549
but I get all excited about it

00:06:00.549 --> 00:06:01.576
because my neighbors are talking about it,

00:06:01.576 --> 00:06:03.865
stocks are going up, everything,

00:06:03.865 --> 00:06:05.485
I start fooling around someplace else,

00:06:05.485 --> 00:06:06.565
eventually I'll get creamed.

00:06:06.565 --> 00:06:07.648
And I should.

00:06:08.737 --> 00:06:12.070
(light piano interlude)

00:06:13.605 --> 00:06:18.465
- A lot of times my issue is
people are not setting goals

00:06:18.465 --> 00:06:22.748
that will be something that,
somebody is becomes rich,

00:06:22.748 --> 00:06:24.562
or could do anything,

00:06:24.562 --> 00:06:28.095
you got to be excited about
working on that for ten years.

00:06:28.095 --> 00:06:31.040
So you got to have a big
enough goal that you know,

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:33.904
that you attract the best
people and you retain them.

00:06:33.904 --> 00:06:35.947
You keep them focused.

00:06:35.947 --> 00:06:37.615
Then in my experience you
don't go wrong, you know,

00:06:37.615 --> 00:06:39.976
maybe you don't hit it next year,

00:06:39.976 --> 00:06:42.927
or maybe you fail entirely
and you discover something

00:06:42.927 --> 00:06:47.785
more exciting to work on
with the same set of people.

00:06:47.785 --> 00:06:51.118
(light piano interlude)

00:06:52.814 --> 00:06:56.659
- Do something you're
very passionate about.

00:06:56.659 --> 00:06:58.050
And

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.550
don't try to chase

00:07:00.646 --> 00:07:04.180
what is kind of the
hot passion of the day.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:06.028
I think we actually saw
this, I think you see it

00:07:06.028 --> 00:07:08.234
all over the place, in
many different contexts,

00:07:08.234 --> 00:07:12.577
I think we saw it in the
internet world quite a bit,

00:07:12.577 --> 00:07:14.623
where the sort of peak

00:07:14.623 --> 00:07:15.873
of the internet

00:07:17.663 --> 00:07:18.734
mania

00:07:18.734 --> 00:07:20.475
in say 1999,

00:07:20.475 --> 00:07:22.475
we found people who were

00:07:23.643 --> 00:07:26.938
you know, very passionate, so
they kind of left that job,

00:07:26.938 --> 00:07:28.172
and decided, you know,

00:07:28.172 --> 00:07:29.572
I'm going to do something on the internet,

00:07:29.572 --> 00:07:33.739
because it was almost like
the 1849 gold rush in a way.

00:07:34.896 --> 00:07:38.333
You find that people, if you
go back and study the history

00:07:38.333 --> 00:07:40.532
of the 1849 gold rush, you find that

00:07:40.532 --> 00:07:44.095
at that time everyone who
was within shouting distance

00:07:44.095 --> 00:07:47.605
of California was, they
might have been a doctor,

00:07:47.605 --> 00:07:50.424
but they quit being a doctor
and started panning for gold!

00:07:50.424 --> 00:07:53.049
(laughter)

00:07:53.049 --> 00:07:55.382
And that almost never works.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:01.254
And even if it does work,
according to some metric,

00:08:01.254 --> 00:08:03.327
financial success or whatever it might be,

00:08:03.327 --> 00:08:06.916
I suspect it leaves you
ultimately unsatisfied.

00:08:06.916 --> 00:08:11.587
So you really need to be
very clear with yourself,

00:08:11.587 --> 00:08:14.039
and I think one of the
best ways to do that

00:08:14.039 --> 00:08:18.174
is this notion of projecting
yourself forward to age 80,

00:08:18.174 --> 00:08:20.641
looking back on your life,
and trying to make sure

00:08:20.641 --> 00:08:22.967
you minimize the number
of regrets you have.

00:08:22.967 --> 00:08:25.263
That works for career decisions,

00:08:25.263 --> 00:08:27.763
it works for family decisions,

00:08:29.082 --> 00:08:33.861
I have a 14 month old son,
and it's very easy for me to,

00:08:33.861 --> 00:08:35.960
if I think about myself when I'm 80,

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:39.710
I know I want to watch
that little guy grow up.

00:08:41.039 --> 00:08:42.296
I don't want to be 80 and think,

00:08:42.296 --> 00:08:44.506
"shoot, I missed that whole thing,

00:08:44.506 --> 00:08:46.138
and I don't have the kind of relationship

00:08:46.138 --> 00:08:49.148
with my son that I wished I
had," and so on and so on.

00:08:49.148 --> 00:08:50.606
So if you think about that,

00:08:50.606 --> 00:08:53.743
so I guess another thing that
I would recommend to people

00:08:53.743 --> 00:08:56.642
is that they always take
a long-term point of view.

00:08:56.642 --> 00:08:58.272
And I think this is something

00:08:58.272 --> 00:09:00.688
about which there is a lot of controversy.

00:09:00.688 --> 00:09:03.154
You know, there's a lot of people,

00:09:03.154 --> 00:09:05.556
and I'm just not one of them,

00:09:05.556 --> 00:09:08.071
believe that you should
just live for the now.

00:09:08.071 --> 00:09:12.151
I think what you do, is you
think about the great expanse

00:09:12.151 --> 00:09:14.230
of time ahead of you, and try to make sure

00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:15.985
that you're planning for that in a way

00:09:15.985 --> 00:09:18.872
that's going to leave
you ultimately satisfied.

00:09:18.872 --> 00:09:22.021
So this is just my, this
is the way it works for me,

00:09:22.021 --> 00:09:22.854
you know what I mean?

00:09:22.854 --> 00:09:26.516
I mean everybody needs to
find that for themself.

00:09:26.516 --> 00:09:30.294
So I think there are a lot
of paths to satisfaction,

00:09:30.294 --> 00:09:33.961
and you need to find
one that works for you.

00:09:35.759 --> 00:09:39.092
(light piano interlude)

00:09:40.314 --> 00:09:41.773
- And I think, you know,

00:09:41.773 --> 00:09:44.673
if you believe in what
you're doing, you know,

00:09:44.673 --> 00:09:48.887
many people will tell
you why you're mistaken,

00:09:48.887 --> 00:09:51.324
why it will never work, you know,

00:09:51.324 --> 00:09:53.862
but if you really believe
passionate what you're doing

00:09:53.862 --> 00:09:57.262
just stand, keep going, keep pushing on,

00:09:57.262 --> 00:09:59.669
keep pushing on, until you either succeed

00:09:59.669 --> 00:10:02.002
or at least you realize that

00:10:04.155 --> 00:10:06.405
it's just not going to happen.

00:10:07.765 --> 00:10:12.130
And then if you don't
succeed, pick yourself up

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:13.785
and try again.

00:10:13.785 --> 00:10:17.673
And ultimately, you know,
if you're that determined,

00:10:17.673 --> 00:10:19.756
you will succeed in life.

00:10:22.457 --> 00:10:26.497
And if you don't, you'll have
a hell of a lot of fun trying.

00:10:26.497 --> 00:10:29.830
(light piano interlude)

00:10:31.432 --> 00:10:32.655
- First off, focus on it.

00:10:32.655 --> 00:10:34.723
Right and I think like, you basically get

00:10:34.723 --> 00:10:36.031
what you spend your time doing.

00:10:36.031 --> 00:10:37.441
Right I mean I want the company

00:10:37.441 --> 00:10:39.269
to build three products this year,

00:10:39.269 --> 00:10:40.613
and we're going to work on a few others

00:10:40.613 --> 00:10:42.041
because people are interested in them,

00:10:42.041 --> 00:10:42.899
but those are the three things

00:10:42.899 --> 00:10:44.654
that I'm spending my product time on.

00:10:44.654 --> 00:10:47.031
You get what you put into it.

00:10:47.031 --> 00:10:50.426
If you spend a third of your time trying

00:10:50.426 --> 00:10:52.746
to make the people around you better,

00:10:52.746 --> 00:10:55.125
through getting better people, mentoring,

00:10:55.125 --> 00:10:56.143
getting them to be better,

00:10:56.143 --> 00:10:57.762
getting the best people in
your organization that you know

00:10:57.762 --> 00:10:59.893
into more impactful roles
and I think over time,

00:10:59.893 --> 00:11:02.532
that just accrues and you
get a better organization.

00:11:02.532 --> 00:11:05.865
(light piano interlude)

00:11:08.634 --> 00:11:10.562
- Think things out for yourself.
Come to your own judgments.

00:11:10.562 --> 00:11:12.229
Don't simply conform

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:16.615
to conventional ways of thinking,

00:11:16.615 --> 00:11:18.338
conventional ways of dressing.

00:11:18.338 --> 00:11:20.808
Conventional ways of acting.

00:11:20.808 --> 00:11:23.891
A lot of things are based on fashion.

00:11:24.822 --> 00:11:27.934
Even morality at times
is based on fashion.

00:11:27.934 --> 00:11:31.767
Slavery was once considered
not to be immoral.

00:11:33.145 --> 00:11:36.999
You know, people are shocked
that the Ancient Greeks

00:11:36.999 --> 00:11:39.931
had slaves, that we had
slavery in this country

00:11:39.931 --> 00:11:43.374
as recently as 130, 140 years ago.

00:11:43.374 --> 00:11:45.332
So there are more moral fads.

00:11:45.332 --> 00:11:46.690
You have to really go
back to first principles

00:11:46.690 --> 00:11:48.830
and think things out for yourself.

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:50.380
Whether they're scientific principles,

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:53.383
or moral principles, or
business ideas or product ideas.

00:11:53.383 --> 00:11:56.525
You have to think things out for yourself.

00:11:56.525 --> 00:11:59.858
(light piano interlude)

00:12:02.001 --> 00:12:04.007
- You know there's one sentence

00:12:04.007 --> 00:12:06.333
that I try to describe Bridgewater by,

00:12:06.333 --> 00:12:11.094
and it's "meaningful work
and meaningful relationships

00:12:11.094 --> 00:12:12.920
is what I want.

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:15.626
Meaningful work and
meaningful relationships

00:12:15.626 --> 00:12:19.513
through radical truth,
and radical transparency."

00:12:19.513 --> 00:12:22.432
So meaningful work means, like
we're in a mission together,

00:12:22.432 --> 00:12:23.866
we're excited about what we do,

00:12:23.866 --> 00:12:26.157
we want to do it excellently,
that's meaningful work.

00:12:26.157 --> 00:12:29.363
Meaningful relationships
are these relationships

00:12:29.363 --> 00:12:32.955
in where there's like, you
know, the honesty helps you.

00:12:32.955 --> 00:12:34.676
You can argue things
through but you always know

00:12:34.676 --> 00:12:37.209
that there's honesty, and
that there's mutual caring

00:12:37.209 --> 00:12:38.158
for each other.

00:12:38.158 --> 00:12:42.445
Radical truth means that
knowing what is true,

00:12:42.445 --> 00:12:44.605
to be able to deal with reality,

00:12:44.605 --> 00:12:46.892
whether you like the reality or not,

00:12:46.892 --> 00:12:49.617
but to know what reality
is, like do you have,

00:12:49.617 --> 00:12:51.381
imagine if you had a disease,

00:12:51.381 --> 00:12:52.862
would you want to know
that you had that disease

00:12:52.862 --> 00:12:54.304
or would you not want to know?

00:12:54.304 --> 00:12:55.339
I'd want to know.

00:12:55.339 --> 00:12:57.641
And so to know, if you have a weakness,

00:12:57.641 --> 00:12:59.162
do you want to know you have the weakness

00:12:59.162 --> 00:13:00.622
or do you not want to know?

00:13:00.622 --> 00:13:03.236
Radical truth and radical transparency

00:13:03.236 --> 00:13:07.460
means you get to see everything.
That's very powerful.

